1
00:00:01,014 --> 00:00:09,199
Welcome to Brazil's states, part 1 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,854 --> 00:00:20,814
We work through 14 of the Brazil regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21,481 --> 00:00:24,291
Let's bring Roraima into view.

4
00:00:30,559 --> 00:00:43,783
Roraima sits in Brazil’s far north-west, inland in the Amazon basin on the
borders with Venezuela and Guyana, with Boa Vista as its capital and Pará and
Amazonas to the south.

5
00:00:44,437 --> 00:00:57,440
Mount Roraima, the tabletop mountain where Brazil, Venezuela and Guyana meet,
is thought to have inspired Conan Doyle’s The Lost World, and its summit is a
plateau of rock and rare plants.

6
00:00:58,105 --> 00:01:05,222
Remember the green-blue peak at the triple border, in the least populated
state in Brazil that the equator runs through.

7
00:01:05,909 --> 00:01:07,999
Let's bring Amapá into view.

8
00:01:13,182 --> 00:01:27,438
Amapá sits in the far north of Brazil on the Atlantic, with French Guiana to
the north and Pará to the south and west. Macapá is its capital, and the old
riverside town of Vila Velha stands near the Amazon mouth.

9
00:01:28,092 --> 00:01:40,712
Its Tumucumaque park is the largest tropical forest national park in the
world, about the size of Belgium, and the equator runs through Macapá straight
down the middle of the football stadium.

10
00:01:41,375 --> 00:01:49,641
Remember the northern state where the equator cuts the football pitch in half,
guarding the largest tropical forest park on earth.

11
00:01:50,321 --> 00:01:52,062
Let's bring Pará into view.

12
00:01:57,749 --> 00:02:08,244
Pará fills the lower Amazon, running from the Atlantic coast at Belém deep
inland along the river, with Amapá to the north and Maranhão and Tocantins to
the south.

13
00:02:08,904 --> 00:02:21,141
Its Carajás mine is the largest iron ore operation on earth, and the Tucuruí
dam above it flooded 2,800 square kilometres of forest when it was filled.

14
00:02:21,792 --> 00:02:29,896
Remember the mouth of the Amazon, wide enough to be called the sea, with the
biggest iron ore mine on earth behind it.

15
00:02:30,586 --> 00:02:33,314
Let's bring Amazonas into view.

16
00:02:38,238 --> 00:02:53,041
Amazonas fills the heart of the Amazon basin, the largest state in Brazil, far
inland from the Atlantic, with Manaus on the Rio Negro and Pará to the east
and Rondônia to the south-west.

17
00:02:53,703 --> 00:03:06,451
At Manaus the dark Rio Negro and the sandy Solimões run side by side for six
kilometres without mixing, because they differ in speed, temperature and
density.

18
00:03:07,111 --> 00:03:14,530
Remember the black river and the sandy river flowing side by side without
mixing, in the biggest state in the country.

19
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:17,778
Let's bring Ceará into view.

20
00:03:26,261 --> 00:03:39,136
Ceará sits on the north-east coast of Brazil, facing the Atlantic, with
Fortaleza, its largest city, on the shore and Piauí, Pernambuco, Paraíba and
Rio Grande do Norte around it.

21
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:51,097
It is the driest populated part of the country, and the droughts of the sertão
here drove waves of migration south and are the subject of much Brazilian
literature.

22
00:03:51,756 --> 00:04:01,265
Remember the dry sertão that once sent people south, and the dunes and steady
wind on the coast that pull kite surfers from far away.

23
00:04:02,066 --> 00:04:04,446
Let's bring Maranhão into view.

24
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:25,759
Maranhão lies on the north-east coast around the great bay where the Amazon
nears the Atlantic, with São Luís, its largest city, on an island and Pará,
Piauí and Tocantins inland.

25
00:04:26,409 --> 00:04:39,586
Its Lençóis Maranhenses are white dunes that fill with rainwater every year
into thousands of blue lagoons, and the French founded its capital São Luís in
1612.

26
00:04:40,249 --> 00:04:48,910
Remember the bay where the Amazon comes near the sea, and the white dunes that
turn into a thousand blue lagoons after the rain.

27
00:04:49,632 --> 00:04:52,395
Let's bring Rio Grande do Norte into view.

28
00:05:00,773 --> 00:05:11,268
Rio Grande do Norte is a narrow strip on the north-east coast, with Natal, its
largest city, facing the Atlantic and Ceará and Paraíba to the west and south.

29
00:05:11,919 --> 00:05:22,798
It produces most of Brazil’s sea salt, and the Pirangi cashew tree near Natal
covers about 8,500 square metres, the largest cashew tree in the world.

30
00:05:23,459 --> 00:05:31,992
Remember the salt state, with the tree so wide it looks like a small forest
and the black dune rising straight out of the beach at Natal.

31
00:05:32,678 --> 00:05:34,721
Let's bring Piauí into view.

32
00:05:40,069 --> 00:05:54,489
Piauí lies in the north-east between Maranhão, Ceará, Pernambuco and Bahia,
with Teresina, its largest city, in the interior and a short Atlantic coast in
the far north.

33
00:05:55,142 --> 00:06:08,493
Serra da Capivara here holds rock paintings that may be 25,000 years old, and
it has the shortest coastline of any Brazilian state, only 66 kilometres.

34
00:06:09,157 --> 00:06:18,840
Remember the state with almost no coastline, and the painted rock shelters in
its sierra that changed what we know about when people reached the Americas.

35
00:06:19,529 --> 00:06:22,164
Let's bring Paraíba into view.

36
00:06:28,532 --> 00:06:39,445
Paraíba is a small north-east state on the Atlantic, with João Pessoa, its
largest city, on the shore and Ceará, Pernambuco and Rio Grande do Norte
around it.

37
00:06:40,101 --> 00:06:49,145
Ponta do Seixas in it is the easternmost point of continental South America,
so the mainland sees the sunrise here before anywhere else in Brazil.

38
00:06:49,802 --> 00:06:58,371
Remember the eastern tip of the continent, where the mainland first catches
the sunrise, with dinosaur footprints in the valley at Sousa.

39
00:06:59,068 --> 00:07:01,703
Let's bring Pernambuco into view.

40
00:07:09,179 --> 00:07:21,672
Pernambuco runs down the east coast of Brazil, with Recife, its largest city,
on the shore and Ceará, Paraíba, Piauí, Bahia and Alagoas around it.

41
00:07:22,329 --> 00:07:31,280
The Dutch held it from 1630 to 1654 and made Recife a planned city with the
first synagogue in the Americas.

42
00:07:31,942 --> 00:07:38,978
Remember the reef coast where the sea breaks, and the frevo and maracatu bands
of Recife carnival.

43
00:07:39,752 --> 00:07:42,562
Let's bring Acre into view.

44
00:07:48,671 --> 00:08:02,406
Acre is the far-western state, deep in the Amazon on the borders with Peru and
Bolivia, with Rio Branco, its largest city, in the middle and Rondônia and
Amazonas to its east.

45
00:08:03,056 --> 00:08:10,811
It was Bolivian territory until rubber tappers from Brazil revolted in 1899,
and Brazil bought it in 1903.

46
00:08:11,471 --> 00:08:22,895
Remember the state Brazil bought after a revolt of rubber tappers, and the
tapper leader Chico Mendes, murdered in 1988 for defending the forest.

47
00:08:23,631 --> 00:08:25,593
Let's bring Tocantins into view.

48
00:08:32,372 --> 00:08:45,503
Tocantins lies in the north-central interior, a wedge reaching north between
Pará and Maranhão and touching Bahia, Piauí, Goiás and Mato Grosso, with
Palmas as its capital.

49
00:08:46,162 --> 00:08:56,356
It is the newest Brazilian state, split from Goiás in 1988, and its capital
Palmas was built from scratch in 1989.

50
00:08:57,009 --> 00:09:06,599
Remember the youngest state, carved out of Goiás with a brand new capital, and
the Jalapão dunes and clear springs in its east.

51
00:09:07,289 --> 00:09:10,052
Let's bring Alagoas into view.

52
00:09:16,214 --> 00:09:31,307
Alagoas is a small east-coast state, with Maceió, its largest city, beside the
coastal lagoons that give the state its name and Pernambuco, Sergipe and Bahia
around it.

53
00:09:31,964 --> 00:09:43,226
Palmares, the great republic of escaped slaves that held out for most of the
1600s, was in its hills, and its leader Zumbi is a national symbol of
resistance.

54
00:09:43,887 --> 00:09:51,434
Remember the lagoon state, where reef pools form at low tide and Zumbi kept a
free republic in the hills.

55
00:09:52,174 --> 00:09:55,065
Let's bring Sergipe into view.

56
00:10:02,090 --> 00:10:11,390
Sergipe is the smallest state in Brazil, squeezed between Alagoas and Bahia on
the east coast, with Aracaju, its largest city, on the shore.

57
00:10:12,054 --> 00:10:26,253
São Cristóvão in it has a square from 1608 that is a UNESCO site, and the São
Francisco river canyon along its border is crossed by boat between cliffs.

58
00:10:26,915 --> 00:10:34,670
Remember the smallest state, with the river canyon sailing past its border and
an old colonial square at São Cristóvão.

59
00:10:52,337 --> 00:11:13,757
14 regions of Brazil, one for each target of the map: Roraima, Amapá, Pará,
Amazonas, Ceará, Maranhão, Rio Grande do Norte, Piauí, Paraíba, Pernambuco,
Acre, Tocantins, Alagoas and Sergipe.

60
00:11:14,424 --> 00:11:23,886
Remember the shape of Brazil from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
